==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
{{calque|en|la|pomum Adami}}, which is found in the botanical sense from 1560 and the anatomical sense from 1600.  According to the earliest sources, the anatomical usage derives from a common belief that Adam was punished by God for the [[w:Fall of man|fall of man]] by having a piece of the [[forbidden fruit]] lodged in his throat (see 1662 citation).

===Noun===
{{en-noun|head=[[Adam]]'s [[apple]]}}

# {{lb|en|anatomy}} The [[lump]] in the [[throat]], usually more [[noticeable]] in [[men]] than in [[women]]; the [[laryngeal prominence]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1662| author=Nicholas Culpeper and Abdiah Cole, transl.| title=Bartholinus Anatomy| pageurl=https://archive.org/details/b30333696/page/122/mode/2up?| publisher=Peter Cole| location=London| page=123| passage=That same bunch which is seen on the foreside of the Neck, is called '''''Adams Apple''''', because the common people have a beliefe, that by the judgement of God, a part of that fatal Apple, abode sticking in ''Adams'' Throat, and is so communicated to his posterity| original=Protuberantia illa in collo anterius conspicua, dicitur ''Pomum Adami''; [quia vulgo persuasum in Adami faucibus pomi fatalis partem ex pœna Divina remansisse, & ad posteros translatam]|by=Thomas Bartholin |origyear=1651}}
#: {{syn|en|laryngeal prominence}}
# {{taxlink|Tabernaemontana divaricata|species|ver=161006}}, a fragrant [[houseplant]] native to southeast Asia.
#: {{syn|en|{{vern|crape jasmine}}|{{vern|pinwheel flower}}|{{vern|Nero's crown}}|{{vern|butterfly gardenia}}|{{vern|carnation of India}}|{{vern|East Indian rosebay}}|{{taxlink|Tabernaemontana coronaria|species|ver=161016}}|{{vern|coffee rose}}|{{vern|crepe gardenia}}}}
# A species of [[banana]] (''[[Musa × paradisiaca]]'').
# The [[citron]], ''[[Citrus limetta]]'', now ''[[Citrus medica]]''.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1597|first=John|last=Gerarde| title=The Herball or Generall Historie of Plantes| url=https://archive.org/details/herballorgeneral00gera_1/page/1280/mode/2up| publisher=John Norton| location=London| page=1281| passage=The fourth [name of the citron] is named of diuers ''Pomum Assyrium'', or Citron of Assyria, and may be Englished '''Adams apple''', after the Italian name, and among the vulgar sort of Italians ''Lomie'', of whom it is also called ''Pomum Adami'', or '''Adams apple''', and that came by the opinion of the commom rude people, who thinke it to be the same Apple, of which ''Adam'' did eate in Paradise when he transgressed Gods commandement}}

